  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nHeading into the latter half of the workweek, ensemble model \nguidance has come into slightly better agreement on the overall \nsynoptic pattern. The models have come into better agreement on \nthe placement of the features, but still differ on the overall \nstrength of the features. Both the GEFS and the EPS show a \nshortwave trough across the northern CONUS and southern Canada, a \nbroad subtropical ridge stretching across much of the lower 2/3 of\nCONUS, along with a closed low off the coast of northern \nCalifornia. The EPS continues to be the slightly stronger \nsolution, with a stronger closed low in the Pacific and a slightly\nstronger subtropical high. This overall better agreement is \nreflected with the slightly smaller temperature spread in the NBM \ninterquartile range, which has narrowed to less than 5 degrees. \nThe GEFS show the high at peak strength (H5 heights of 594-597 dm)\non Wednesday before weakening (H5 heights of 591-594 dm) by the \nlatter half of the week. The high looks to continue to weaken (H5\nheights of 588-592 dm) heading into the weekend. Therefore, \ntemperatures are forecasted to remain about 5 degrees above normal\nduring the middle of the week before beginning a slight cooling \ntrend for the end of the workweek and into the weekend closer to \nnear normal levels. \n\nAs for shower and storm chances, the ensemble members continue to \nshow those chances starting to increase, for at least portions of \nthe CWA, beginning on Wednesday. PWATs look to increase to around \n1.5-1.9\" for the latter half of the week, so moisture should not be \na limiting factor. With the high forecasted to move overhead, \nrain chances will likely be limited to the higher terrain areas \nacross eastern Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday. Rain chances \nmay extend into the lower deserts of south-central Arizona heading \ninto the weekend. However, the current WPC PoPs of 30-40%, for \nFriday into Saturday, seem rather aggressive, especially this far \nout. WPC's Saturday PoPs of 10-30% seem more reasonable.",
